  summary: [
    "Phospholipids spontaneously assemble into a bilayer in water, hydrophilic heads facing outward and hydrophobic tails shielded inside, forming the thin (4&ndash;10 nm) but stable barrier common to every cell membrane.",
    "Diffusion is the passive movement of particles from high to low concentration, powered by the particles&rsquo; own kinetic energy; small non-polar molecules such as O<sub>2</sub> and CO<sub>2</sub> diffuse straight across the phospholipid bilayer.",
    "Osmosis is the diffusion of water across a partially permeable membrane, from a region of lower solute (higher water) concentration to a region of higher solute (lower water) concentration; it occurs both through aquaporins and through transient gaps between phospholipid molecules.",
    "Integral proteins are embedded within the bilayer, while peripheral proteins attach only to its surface; channel proteins (a type of integral protein) allow facilitated diffusion &mdash; passive movement of polar or charged particles down their concentration gradient, requiring no ATP.",
    "Active transport moves particles against a concentration gradient using pump proteins powered by ATP (from respiration); it is selective, since most pumps are specific to one substance, as shown by the differing, temperature-dependent uptake of Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> by plant tissue.",
    "Permeability by simple diffusion is unselective, depending only on particle size and polarity; facilitated diffusion and active transport, which rely on specific proteins, make membranes selectively permeable instead.",
    "Glycoproteins and glycolipids on the outer membrane surface form the glycocalyx, involved in cell recognition and adhesion; together with phospholipids and proteins, they make up the fluid mosaic model (Singer and Nicolson, 1972), which replaced the earlier, falsified Davson&ndash;Danielli model of a continuous protein-coated bilayer.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> membrane fluidity depends on fatty acid saturation (unsaturated tails pack less closely and melt at lower temperatures) and on cholesterol, which modulates fluidity by disturbing phospholipid packing; organisms such as the lake sturgeon adjust this balance (homeoviscous adaptation) to stay functional at low temperatures.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> bulk transport (cytosis) moves vesicles of solid or liquid matter using ATP and the flexibility of the fluid mosaic membrane &mdash; endocytosis (including phagocytosis and pinocytosis) takes matter in, while exocytosis exports it, as in antibody secretion or neurotransmitter release.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> gated ion channels in neurons open either in response to a neurotransmitter (neurotransmitter-gated, e.g. the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor) or to a change in membrane voltage (voltage-gated, e.g. the Na<sup>+</sup> and K<sup>+</sup> channels of an action potential, closed again in part by a &lsquo;ball and chain&rsquo; mechanism).",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> the sodium&ndash;potassium pump exchanges 3 Na<sup>+</sup> out for 2 K<sup>+</sup> in per cycle using ATP directly; sodium-dependent glucose cotransporters instead use the Na<sup>+</sup> gradient this pump creates to move glucose into cells against its own gradient &mdash; indirect active transport.",
    "<strong>HL:</strong> cell-adhesion molecules (cadherins and integrins) bind animal cells to each other or to the extracellular matrix, forming anchoring, tight, gap and signal-relaying junctions; plant cells instead rely on interlocking cell walls, with plasmodesmata (rather than gap junctions) connecting adjacent cytoplasm."
  ]
